WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.810
Safe party food: the Five Keys and the danger zone.

2
00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:10.470
This is the video for module one of Catering Basics and Food Safety: safe catering foundations.

3
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:21.060
Catering is cooking for people who trust you with their health. It adds three things to good cooking. Scale: you cook five or ten times more than usual.

4
00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:29.080
Responsibility: the guests are not your family, and some may be elderly, pregnant, very young or allergic.

5
00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:34.960
And time pressure: the food must be ready, hot and safe at an exact time.

6
00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:41.280
In this video you will learn the rules that keep rice, stew and chicken safe for hours.

7
00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:57.420
Key one. Keep clean. Wash hands with soap and clean running water before handling food, and after the toilet, raw meat, money or rubbish. Anyone with diarrhoea, vomiting or an infected cut should not handle food that day.

8
00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:07.420
Key two. Separate raw and cooked. Use separate boards and knives for raw meat, and store raw meat covered, below cooked food in the fridge.

9
00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:15.220
Key three. Cook thoroughly. Meat, poultry and eggs must reach 70 degrees Celsius at the centre.

10
00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:21.230
Key four. Keep food at safe temperatures. We will look at this closely in a moment.

11
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:32.480
Key five. Use safe, treated water and good raw materials. Reject swollen or dented cans, mouldy grains and food past its expiry date.

12
00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:46.620
For example, Funmi caters a naming ceremony for 60 guests. She uses a red board for raw chicken and a green board only for cabbage and carrots, and keeps the coleslaw covered on a separate table.

13
00:01:47.870 --> 00:02:00.970
The common mistake is using the same board for raw chicken and then for coleslaw cabbage, or reusing the marinade bowl for cooked chicken. Coleslaw is not cooked, so nothing kills germs that land on it.

14
00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:10.690
Bacteria grow fastest at warm temperatures, like a Nigerian afternoon. First, cook meat to 70 degrees at the centre.

15
00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:16.610
Keep rice, stew and meat steaming hot, above 60 degrees, until served.

16
00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:22.130
Keep coleslaw, salad and cream desserts chilled, below 5 degrees.

17
00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:27.260
Never leave cooked food at room temperature for more than 2 hours.

18
00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:42.740
Reheat only once, until steaming hot all through. The danger zone is between 5 and 60 degrees. Unsafe food can look, smell and taste normal, so decide by time and temperature, never by tasting.

19
00:02:43.990 --> 00:03:00.260
Cooked rice needs special care. Rice can carry spores that cooking does not kill. If cooked rice sits warm for hours, they make toxins that reheating does not destroy. So, to cool food, spread it into shallow trays, not one deep pot.

20
00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:04.910
Stand the containers in a basin of cold water with ice.

21
00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:12.410
When steam stops rising, cover and refrigerate, below 5 degrees, well within the 2-hour limit.

22
00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:18.390
Label each container with the dish and date. And when in doubt, throw it out.

23
00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:29.800
Chioma has two insulated coolers and a probe thermometer. She warms each cooler with hot water for 10 minutes first, so the cold walls do not chill the food.

24
00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:39.860
At 10:30 am the rice reads 85 degrees and the chicken 78. She fills the coolers hot and closes them at once.

25
00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:46.850
At 12:15 pm at the venue, the rice still reads 68 degrees, above 60.

26
00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:59.920
At 3:30 pm the leftover rice reads 52, below 60, so she does not take it home for resale. She writes every reading on a log sheet as evidence of safe practice.

27
00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:17.460
To remember. Apply the Five Keys to every dish. Keep raw and ready food apart. Cook to 70 degrees, hold above 60 or below 5. Never more than 2 hours at room temperature. And use a probe thermometer and write the readings down.

28
00:04:18.710 --> 00:04:32.300
Now practise. Map your kitchen capacity: your pots, burners, helpers and transport. Do a Five Keys audit and fix two gaps this week. And test how long your cooler really keeps food above 60 degrees.

29
00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:34.600
Continue with the lessons below.
